var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#BGK-Equation","page":"BGK","text":"BGKEquation is a discrete-velocity model of the Bhatnagar–Gross–Krook equation\n\npartial_t f + vec vcdotnabla_x f = frac1taubig(mathcal Mf - fbig)\n\nfor the distribution f(vec x vec v t) of a gas in D space dimensions. Its moments U = (rho rhovec u E) are the conservative variables of the Euler equations, which the BGK solution approaches for tau to 0; for tau to infty every velocity moves freely.","title":"BGK Equation"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#Velocity-space-as-the-state-vector","page":"BGK","text":"The velocity space is discretised by a tensor grid of Nv nodes per direction (velocity_grid), and the particle state is the vector of distribution values at the nodes: the number of equations is the number of discrete velocities. Everything HyperCloudCore offers (MUSCL, limiters, MOOD, IMEX, moving grids, the device backends) therefore runs on the BGK model unchanged. Moments are quadrature sums, U = sum_k w_k(1 vec v_k tfrac12vec v_k^2)f_k.\n\nSince the state is an SVector, its length drives compile time and per-particle cost. The model keeps it small in three ways:\n\nOnly D velocity directions. Degrees of freedom beyond the resolved velocity directions (the remaining directions of a 3-D gas and internal ones, K = 2(gamma - 1) - D in total) are carried by a second distribution h = int tfrac12xi^2 fdxi on the same nodes (a Chu reduction). The state is f for gamma = (D + 2)D (M = N_v^D), otherwise (f h) (M = 2 N_v^D). A 1-D shock tube with gamma = 14 needs a 1-D velocity grid, not N_v^3 velocities.\nGauss–Hermite nodes (:velocity_grid => :gauss_hermite) integrate polynomials times the Gaussian of their scale exactly, so near-equilibrium flows need far fewer nodes than the uniform midpoint grid (:uniform, the robust choice for strongly non-equilibrium flows).\nNo per-velocity arrays in kernels. The equation is a small bits type (1-D nodes and weights, grid centre, K, gamma, relaxation model); the node of a component is resolved from its index at compile time, so the flux and the dissipation unroll into straight-line code.","title":"Velocity space as the state vector"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#Collision-term","page":"BGK","text":"The equilibrium is the conservative discrete Maxwellian of equilibrium (f_k = exp(alphacdotpsi_k) with psi = (1 vec v tfrac12vec v^2), Mieussens' discrete entropic equilibrium, h_k = tfrac K2hattheta f_k): alpha is found by a damped Newton iteration on the convex dual problem, started from the continuous Maxwellian, so the discrete moments of the equilibrium equal U to round-off and the collision term conserves mass, momentum and energy exactly. A few microseconds per particle; the iteration runs inside the kernels.\n\nThe relaxation time comes from a RelaxationTimeModel: constant (ConstantRelaxationTime, Inf for free transport) or tau = mu(theta)p with a power-law viscosity (ViscosityRelaxationTime).\n\nWith an IMEX tableau the collision is the implicit source BGKRelaxation. Because it conserves the moments, the implicit stage equation has the closed-form solution f = (f^* + lambdamathcal Mf^*)(1 + lambda), lambda = Delta ta_iitau, for any stiffness, which makes the scheme asymptotic preserving: at tau = 10^-5 the BGK Sod solution is as close to the exact Euler solution as the Euler scheme itself. With an explicit tableau the collision becomes ExplicitBGKRelaxation (stable for Delta t lesssim tau only).","title":"Collision term"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#Transport","page":"BGK","text":"The flux is v_kd f_k. The transport is linear and diagonal, so the Rusanov flux of every velocity is its exact upwind flux: RusanovFlux, UpwindFlux and AlignedRusanovFlux use the dissipation v_kd per component instead of the largest node speed for all components (which smears the slow velocities; on a smooth collisionless wave it costs a factor 2 to 10 in the error). max_eigenvalue is the largest node speed, which sets the CFL time step.\n\nThe PAD of MOOD (is_admissible) asks for positive density and temperature; individual f_k may dip below zero.","title":"Transport"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#Configuration","page":"BGK","text":":PDE_name => :bgk, :PDE_D => 1, :PDE_gamma => 1.4,\n:PDE_Nv => 24, :PDE_vmax => 6.0, :PDE_velocity_grid => :uniform,\n:PDE_tau => 1e-3,                       # or :PDE_viscosity => μ_ref, :PDE_omega => ω\n:sim_func_name => :run_bgk_simulation,  # direct and kinetic runners forward BGK runs here\n\nInitial conditions give the macroscopic state (rho rhovec u E), exactly as for an Euler run with the same gamma; every particle starts in the discrete Maxwellian of that state. The stored snapshots are the moments, so BGK and Euler runs can be compared directly (:save_relax => true keeps the distributions). Choose vmax so that the Maxwellians of the flow decay inside the grid (u + 5sqrttheta or more) and enough nodes to resolve them (Delta v lesssim sqrttheta).\n\nReferences: the collisionless model has the exact solution of analytic_closure (each velocity transported, initial data in equilibrium); for small tau use euler_limit_solution as the reference of a SimulationConfig (see the example bgk_1d_sod).","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#Cost-of-the-velocity-space","page":"BGK","text":"Measured on 8 threads (ARS222, MUSCL3 with the Venkatakrishnan limiter, regular lattice, Float64), one time step and the first (compiling) step:\n\nD M particles step / particle first step per-particle buffers neighbour-slot buffers\n1 16 4000 0.32 µs 3 s 1.9 KiB 10 × 0.27 KiB\n1 64 4000 1.6 µs 5 s 6.8 KiB 10 × 1.0 KiB\n1 128 4000 3.2 µs 11 s 13 KiB 10 × 2.0 KiB\n1 256 4000 7.1 µs 30 s 26 KiB 10 × 4.0 KiB\n2 16 64² 2.4 µs 5 s 3.1 KiB 36 × 0.41 KiB\n2 64 64² 5.8 µs 15 s 9.1 KiB 36 × 1.5 KiB\n2 144 64² 16 µs 40 s 19 KiB 36 × 3.4 KiB\n2 256 64² 30 µs 95 s 33 KiB 36 × 6.0 KiB\n\nThe cost per particle grows linearly with M up to a few hundred equations. In 2-D the per-slot buffers of the scheme (the flux differences of every neighbour, (D + 1) M values per slot) dominate the memory, about 6× the per-particle buffers, and the compile time of the first step grows faster than M; 16 times 16 velocities still work (95 s to compile, 30 µs per particle and step). About a third of a step is the collision (two equilibria per stage), a third the gradient pre-pass with the limiter, and the rest the flux pass.","title":"Cost of the velocity space"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/bgk/#API","page":"BGK","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.BGKEquation","page":"BGK","text":"BGKEquation{D, M, T, NV1, NV, R} <: HyperbolicPDE{D, M, T}\nBGKEquation(::Val{D}, nodes, weights, gamma, relaxation; center = 0)\n\nDiscrete-velocity model of the BGK equation ∂_t f + v·∇f = (M[f] - f) / τ in D space dimensions. The velocity space is the tensor product of the 1-D nodes (with quadrature weights, see velocity_grid) in every direction, shifted by center: NV = NV1^D discrete velocities.\n\nThe state of a particle is the vector of distribution values at the nodes, so the number of equations is the number of velocities. Degrees of freedom beyond the D resolved velocity directions (the remaining velocity directions of a 3-D gas and internal ones, K = 2 / (γ - 1) - D in total) are carried by a second distribution h (a Chu reduction, h = ∫ |ξ|²/2 f dξ over the unresolved directions): for K = 0 (γ = (D + 2) / D) the state is f (M = NV), otherwise (f, h) (M = 2 NV). Any γ thus needs only a D-dimensional velocity grid.\n\nThe macroscopic state is U = (ρ, ρu..., E) = Σ_k w_k (1, v_k, |v_k|²/2) f_k + (0, 0, h_k) (see moments), the conservative variables of the Euler equations with the same γ (euler_limit), which the BGK solution approaches for τ → 0. The collision term relaxes towards the conservative discrete Maxwellian of equilibrium (source terms BGKRelaxation, ExplicitBGKRelaxation) with the relaxation time of relaxation (a RelaxationTimeModel).\n\nThe transport is linear and diagonal (velocity k moves with v_k): the flux is v_{k,d} f_k, the Rusanov and Upwind fluxes reduce to exact upwinding per velocity (dissipation |v_{k,d}| instead of the largest speed for every component), and max_eigenvalue is the largest node speed. The equation object is a small bits type (nodes, weights, center, K, γ, the relaxation model); node indices are resolved at compile time, so there are no per-velocity arrays in the kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.BGKEquation"},{"category":"method","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloudCore.is_admissible-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{BGKEquation{D, M, T}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{M, T}}} where {D, M, T}","page":"BGK","text":"Admissible BGK state (MOOD PAD): finite, with positive density and temperature (individual f_k may dip below zero).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloudCore.is_admissible"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.velocity_grid","page":"BGK","text":"velocity_grid(kind::Symbol, Nv::Int, vmax::Real, ::Type{T} = Float64) -> (nodes, weights)\n\nOne-dimensional velocity nodes and quadrature weights (SVector{Nv, T} each) of a discrete-velocity model; the BGK equation uses their tensor product in every spatial direction.\n\n:uniform: midpoint rule on [-vmax, vmax], v_j = -vmax + (j - 1/2) Δv, weights Δv = 2 vmax / Nv. Spectrally accurate for Maxwellians that decay inside the interval; the robust choice for strongly non-equilibrium flows.\n:gauss_hermite: Gauss–Hermite nodes scaled so that the outermost node is ±vmax, with the weights w_j e^{x_j^2} that integrate f itself. Exact for polynomials times the Gaussian of that scale, so near-equilibrium flows need far fewer nodes (the conservative equilibrium keeps the moments exact either way). Weights come from the Christoffel function of the Hermite functions (stable for large Nv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.velocity_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.num_velocities","page":"BGK","text":"num_velocities(eq::BGKEquation) -> Int\n\nNumber of discrete velocities NV = NV1^D (the number of equations is NV, or 2 NV with internal degrees of freedom).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.num_velocities"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.velocity_node","page":"BGK","text":"velocity_node(eq::BGKEquation, k::Int) -> (v::Space{D, T}, w::T)\n\nVelocity and quadrature weight of state component k (components NV + 1 … 2 NV of h share the nodes of f).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.velocity_node"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.moments","page":"BGK","text":"moments(eq::BGKEquation, f::State) -> State{D + 2}\n\nMacroscopic conservative state (ρ, ρu..., E) of the distribution f by quadrature over the velocity grid (E includes the energy Σ w_k h_k of the unresolved degrees of freedom).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.moments"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.equilibrium","page":"BGK","text":"equilibrium(eq::BGKEquation, U::State{D + 2}) -> (f_eq::State{M}, ok::Bool)\n\nConservative discrete Maxwellian of the macroscopic state U = (ρ, ρu..., E): the discrete entropic equilibrium f_k = exp(α·ψ_k) (Mieussens), whose discrete moments reproduce U to round-off on the velocity grid, so the collision term conserves mass, momentum and energy exactly. α is found by a damped Newton iteration on the convex dual problem, started from the continuous Maxwellian (on a grid that resolves the Maxwellian that start is already accurate to the quadrature error, so one or two iterations suffice). With internal degrees of freedom h_k = (K/2) θ̂ f_k.\n\nok is false for a non-physical U (non-finite, ρ ≤ 0 or θ ≤ 0); f_eq is meaningless then. If the grid cannot represent U (e.g. a mean velocity near vmax), the iteration stops at the closest equilibrium it reaches. Allocation free, runs inside kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.equilibrium"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.euler_limit","page":"BGK","text":"euler_limit(eq::BGKEquation) -> EulerEquation\n\nThe compressible Euler equations (conservative variables) with the ratio of specific heats of the BGK model, which its moments approach for τ → 0: the reference for small relaxation times.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.euler_limit"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.RelaxationTimeModel","page":"BGK","text":"RelaxationTimeModel\n\nRelaxation time τ(ρ, θ) of the BGK collision operator (M[f] - f) / τ, evaluated from the macroscopic density ρ and temperature θ = p / ρ of a particle. Subtypes implement relaxation_time(model, ρ, θ); it runs inside kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.RelaxationTimeModel"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.ConstantRelaxationTime","page":"BGK","text":"ConstantRelaxationTime(tau)\n\nConstant relaxation time τ (the Knudsen number of the non-dimensional BGK equation). τ = Inf is the collisionless (free transport) limit: the runners drop the collision term and analytic_closure returns the exact solution of the discrete-velocity model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.ConstantRelaxationTime"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.ViscosityRelaxationTime","page":"BGK","text":"ViscosityRelaxationTime(mu_ref, omega)\n\nτ = μ(θ) / p with the power-law viscosity μ = mu_ref θ^ω (the Chapman–Enskog viscosity of the BGK model is τ p), i.e. τ = mu_ref θ^(ω - 1) / ρ. ω = 1/2 are hard spheres, ω = 1 Maxwell molecules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.ViscosityRelaxationTime"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.relaxation_time","page":"BGK","text":"relaxation_time(model::RelaxationTimeModel, ρ, θ)\nrelaxation_time(eq::BGKEquation, U::State)\n\nRelaxation time of the BGK collision term for density ρ and temperature θ, or for the macroscopic state U = (ρ, ρu..., E) of the equation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.relaxation_time"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.BGKRelaxation","page":"BGK","text":"BGKRelaxation(eq::BGKEquation) <: AbstractImplicitSourceTerm\nExplicitBGKRelaxation(eq::BGKEquation) <: AbstractExplicitSourceTerm\n\nThe BGK collision term S(f) = (M[f] - f) / τ with the conservative discrete Maxwellian M[f] of equilibrium and τ from the equation's RelaxationTimeModel, treated implicitly (IMEX tableaus) or explicitly (explicit RK tableaus, only stable for Δt ≲ τ).\n\nThe implicit stage equation f = f* + Δt a_ii S(f) is solved in closed form: the collision conserves the moments, so M[f] = M[f*] and τ(f) = τ(f*), and f = (f* + λ M[f*]) / (1 + λ) with λ = Δt a_ii / τ. This is exact for any stiffness (τ → 0 projects onto the equilibrium), which makes the IMEX scheme asymptotic preserving towards the Euler equations. A non-physical particle state (ρ ≤ 0 or θ ≤ 0) is left untouched (zero source).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.BGKRelaxation"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.ExplicitBGKRelaxation","page":"BGK","text":"The explicit form of the BGK collision term, see BGKRelaxation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.ExplicitBGKRelaxation"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.run_bgk_simulation","page":"BGK","text":"run_bgk_simulation(params::ParamDict) -> Union{AbstractSimData, Nothing}\n\nRuns the discrete-velocity BGK model (:PDE_name => :bgk, see BGKEquation). The particle state is the distribution at the velocity nodes; the IC namespace gives the macroscopic state (ρ, ρu..., E) (initial conditions are built with M = D + 2, like an Euler run with the same γ), and every particle starts in its discrete Maxwellian. The collision term is implicit for an IMEX tableau (BGKRelaxation), explicit for an explicit one (ExplicitBGKRelaxation) and dropped in the collisionless limit (:PDE_tau => Inf). Schemes, fluxes, limiters, MOOD and the grid are configured exactly as for the other equations.\n\nThe returned snapshots hold the macroscopic moments (ρ, ρu..., E), directly comparable with Euler runs; with :save_relax => true they keep the full distributions instead. run_direct_simulation and run_kinetic_simulation forward BGK configurations here, so the method dictionaries work unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.run_bgk_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.euler_limit_solution","page":"BGK","text":"euler_limit_solution(params::ParamDict)\n\nReference for BGK runs (:PDE_name => :bgk) in the hydrodynamic limit τ → 0: the exact solution of the Euler equations with the same :PDE_D, :PDE_gamma (default (D + 2) / D, as for the BGK model), initial condition and domain, i.e. analytical_solution with :PDE_name => :euler. Pass it as the reference of a SimulationConfig; it accepts any parameter set with an Euler-compatible PDE namespace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.euler_limit_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/bgk/#HyperCloud.bgk_1d_sod","page":"BGK","text":"bgk_1d_sod()\n\nThe Sod shock tube for the discrete-velocity BGK model (γ = 1.4, i.e. a Chu-reduced distribution pair on a 1-D velocity grid of 24 nodes, 48 equations) at the relaxation times τ = 1e-5, 1e-3, 1e-2, against the exact Euler solution (euler_limit_solution), which the BGK solution approaches as τ → 0. Methods: IMEX ARS222 with MUSCL2 (Venkatakrishnan limiter, or U2 MOOD) and explicit RK2 with MUSCL2 (the collision term explicit, only stable for the larger τ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.bgk_1d_sod"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/advection/#Linear-Advection","page":"Linear Advection","text":"LinearAdvection implements coupled linear transport with constant matrices:\n\nfracpartial Upartial t + sum_d=1^D A_d fracpartial Upartial x_d = 0\n\nwhere U has M components and every A_d is an M times M matrix.","title":"Linear Advection"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/advection/#Specifying-the-velocities","page":"Linear Advection","text":"velocities has one entry per dimension, each describing A_d. The dimension D is the number of entries.\n\nLinearAdvection(((1.0,),), Float64)               # 1D scalar: u_t + u_x = 0\nLinearAdvection(((1.0,), (0.5,)), Float64)        # 2D scalar, velocity (1, 0.5)\nLinearAdvection(((1.0, -1.0),), Float64)          # 1D, two variables: diag(1, -1)\n\nA tuple of M numbers is read as the diagonal matrix operatornamediag(a_1 dots a_M). This is the format produced by generate_relax_params for kinetic systems. Other formats are converted by param2vel.\n\nIn a configuration: :PDE_name => :linear, :PDE_velocities => ((1.0,), (1.0,)).","title":"Specifying the velocities"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/advection/#Implementation","page":"Linear Advection","text":"Wave speeds. The constructor precomputes the spectral radius of every A_d; max_eigenvalue returns it without any work at run time.\nVariables. prim2cons and cons2prim are the identity.\nMoving grids. For scalar equations (M = 1), fluid_velocity returns the advection velocity; a Lagrangian grid then carries the solution exactly.\nFlux. F_d(U) = A_d U; velocity(eq, U, d) returns A_d.\nKinetic use. kinetic_wave_speed(eq, d, k) returns the diagonal entry (A_d)_kk. It is meaningful for diagonal systems, which is how kinetic relaxation systems are built.","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/advection/#Exact-solution","page":"Linear Advection","text":"The exact solution works with any initial condition and any dimension:\n\nCommutation check. For D  1 the matrices must commute (A_i A_j = A_j A_i). Otherwise the closure throws an error.\nCharacteristic variables. It diagonalizes sum_d A_d = R Lambda R^-1. The speed of characteristic m in direction d is lambda_md = (R^-1 A_d R)_mm.\nTracing. Each characteristic variable is the initial condition at x - lambda_m t, projected with R^-1. Periodic directions are wrapped into the domain. The physical state is reassembled with R.\n\nnote: Note\nStep 2 assumes that the eigenvectors of sum_d A_d also diagonalize every A_d. This always holds for diagonal systems. It can fail for non-diagonal matrices when sum_d A_d has repeated eigenvalues.","title":"Exact solution"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/advection/#API","page":"Linear Advection","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/advection/#HyperCloud.LinearAdvection","page":"Linear Advection","text":"LinearAdvection{D, M, T, L} <: HyperbolicPDE{D, M, T}\n\nImplements N-dimensional, M-variable coupled linear advection systems. \n\nFields\n\nvel::Velocity{D, M, T, L}: The constant advective velocity matrices for each spatial dimension.\nmax_eigs::SVector{D, T}: The precomputed spectral radius (maximum absolute eigenvalue) for each spatial matrix, used to optimize runtime execution.\n\nConstructors\n\nLinearAdvection(velocities, ::Type{T}=eltype(velocities[1])): Automatically extracts the spatial dimension D and system size M from the provided velocity matrices and precomputes the exact spectral radii.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.LinearAdvection"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/main/#PDE-Architecture-and-Exact-Solutions","page":"Architecture","text":"Every equation is a struct subtyping HyperbolicPDE{D, M, T}, where D is the spatial dimension, M the number of variables and T the floating-point type. The numerical schemes only access an equation through a small set of functions, so new physics plugs in without touching the solver. The full contract, including which scheme needs which method, is HyperCloudCore's API Contracts.","title":"PDE Architecture & Exact Solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/main/#Equation-interface","page":"Architecture","text":"Function Required Returns\nflux(eq, U) yes Flux{D, M, T}: the physical flux F_d(U) for every direction d.\nmax_eigenvalue(eq, U, d) yes Largest absolute wave speed in direction d (used for the CFL step and Rusanov flux).\nprim2cons(eq, V), cons2prim(eq, U) yes Conversion between primitive and conservative variables (identity for scalar laws).\nvelocity(eq, U, d) upwind schemes, WENO, UpwindFlux Flux Jacobian partial F_d  partial U as an M × M static matrix.\nfluid_velocity(eq, U) Lagrangian grid movers Velocity the particles move with, a Space{D, T}.\nis_admissible(eq, U) optional Physical admissibility for MOOD (default: all components finite).\nmax_eigenvalue(eq, U, d, shift) optional Exact relative wave speed on moving grids.\nkinetic_wave_speed(eq, d, k) kinetic systems Speed of kinetic variable k in direction d.\nanalytic_closure(eq, ic, geom) optional Exact solution, see below.\n\nThe functions run inside the time step, on a GPU as device code, so they have to follow HyperCloudCore's rules for kernel bodies. Import them from HyperCloudCore before adding methods (import HyperCloudCore: flux, max_eigenvalue).\n\nA builder method makes the equation available from configurations:\n\nfunction HyperCloud.build_equation(::Val{:my_pde}, pde_conf::Dict, context::Dict)\n    T = context[:T]::DataType\n    return MyPDE{pde_conf[:D], T}()\nend","title":"Equation interface"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/main/#Representations","page":"Architecture","text":"Some equations exist in several formulations. They carry a representation tag, a subtype of EquationRepresentation. Conservative is the conservative form; NCRepresentation covers non-conservative (e.g. primitive) forms, whose integration paths are configured through parse_representation. Methods such as flux and max_eigenvalue dispatch on the tag.","title":"Representations"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/main/#Exact-solutions","page":"Architecture","text":"analytical_solution builds the equation, domain and initial condition from a configuration and calls analytic_closure. The result is a closure\n\nexact = analytical_solution(params)\nexact(SVector(x..., t))   # -> State{M}\n\nthat evaluates the exact solution at any spacetime point. It is used as the reference for error statistics and plots. The reference is computed in params[:real_type] (default Float64), independently of the simulation precision.\n\nExact solutions currently available:\n\nEquation Initial condition Dimension\nLinearAdvection any any D (commuting velocity matrices)\nBurgersEquation Riemann, SRiemann any D\nBurgersEquation Gauss any D, before the breaking time\nBurgersEquation Box 1D\nEulerEquation Riemann 1D\n\nOther combinations fall back to a warning. Burgers returns the initial condition (exact only at t = 0); every other equation returns nothing.\n\nTo add an exact solution, add a method for your combination:\n\nfunction HyperCloud.analytic_closure(eq::MyPDE{D, T}, ic::Gauss, geom::GeometricDomain) where {D, T}\n    # precompute everything that does not depend on (x, t) here\n    return function (st::SVector)\n        t = T(st[end])\n        x = SVector{D, T}(ntuple(d -> T(st[d]), Val(D)))\n        # ...\n        return State{1, T}(u)\n    end\nend\n\ngeom gives access to the domain bounds (geom.mins, geom.maxs) and periodicity (geom.is_periodic).","title":"Exact solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/main/#API","page":"Architecture","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/main/#HyperCloud.analytical_solution","page":"Architecture","text":"analytical_solution(shared_params::ParamDict)\n\nBuilds the equation, domain and initial condition from the PDE, Grid and IC namespaces and returns the standalone closure exact_u(st) from analytic_closure. st is the spacetime vector SVector(x..., t).\n\nThe reference is computed in shared_params[:real_type] (default Float64), independently of the simulation precision :T, so Float32 runs are still compared to a Float64 reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.analytical_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"PDEs/main/#HyperCloud.analytic_closure","page":"Architecture","text":"analytic_closure(eq::BGKEquation, ic::InitialCondition, geom::GeometricDomain)\n\nFor the collisionless model (ConstantRelaxationTime(Inf)) the exact solution of the discrete-velocity equation: every velocity is transported, f_k(x, t) = f_k^0(x - v_k t) with the equilibrium initial data of ic (wrapped on periodic axes), and the closure returns its moments (ρ, ρu..., E) like the BGK runner. Finite relaxation times have no closed-form solution (warns, returns nothing); compare with the Euler limit analytic_closure(euler_limit(eq), ic, geom) for small τ instead.\n\n\n\n\n\nanalytic_closure(eq::HyperbolicPDE, ic::InitialCondition, geom::GeometricDomain)\n\nReturns a closure st -> State{M} evaluating the exact solution at the spacetime point st = SVector(x..., t). PDEs add methods for the initial conditions they can solve exactly.\n\nThis generic fallback only emits a warning and returns nothing when no method exists for the combination of equation, initial condition and domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.analytic_closure"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/main/#HyperCloud.EquationRepresentation","page":"Architecture","text":"EquationRepresentation\n\nAbstract base type for categorizing the mathematical formulation of a PDE system.\n\nSubtypes\n\nConservative: Concrete type indicating the system evaluates standard conservative fluxes and eigenvalues.\nNCRepresentation: Abstract type for non-conservative evaluations (e.g., primitive or Lagrangian representations).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.EquationRepresentation"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#Simulation-Engine","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"The runners turn a flat parameter dictionary into a complete solver, run it, and package the result: run_direct_simulation for the PDE itself, run_kinetic_simulation for its kinetic relaxation approximation, and run_bgk_simulation for the discrete-velocity BGK model.","title":"Simulation Engine"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#Pipeline","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"Both runners follow the same steps, sharing one context dictionary (see Builder API; the numerical builders are HyperCloudCore's config-driven builders):\n\nSplit the parameters into namespaces with extract_namespace (PDE, Grid, Weight, Flux, Limiter, MOOD, Scheme, IC, Time, Adapt, and Kinetic for the kinetic runner).\nBuild the equation. It determines the dimension D and system size M.\nBuild the geometry and particle grid, including the MLS weight function.\nBuild the spatial discretization: flux, limiter, MOOD and scheme.\nBuild the time stepper, HyperCloudCore's GeneralRKTimeStepper, from the Butcher tableau (explicit RK or IMEX), and optionally the particle adaptivity (Adapt namespace).\nApply the initial condition.\nIntegrate with solve_equation up to tmax, using either a fixed dt or a CFL number.\nPackage the snapshots into an AbstractSimData and add the statistics Runtime (wall-clock seconds), k_step (number of steps) and final_particle_count, plus reserved_particles and reserved_bytes with a memory limit.\n\nThe simulation precision is params[:T] (default Float64).\n\nThe returned object holds one entry per snapshot: sim.x[k] are the positions, sim.u[k] the states and sim.t[k] the time of snapshot k.\n\nwarning: Errors are caught\nBoth runners catch every exception, log it with @error (including the backtrace), and return nothing. Always check the result, e.g. @assert sim !== nothing, when calling them directly.","title":"Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#Running-on-a-GPU","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"The runners place a run where HyperCloudCore's execution backend runs (storage_for; see Execution backends). The CPU backends (PolyesterBackend(), the default, ThreadsBackend() and KernelAbstractions.CPU()) keep the run in host memory. With a GPU backend, the grid, the scheme and the time stepper live on the device (context[:Storage]). No parameter changes, so a GPU run has the same parameter hash as its CPU counterpart:\n\nusing HyperCloud, AMDGPU          # or CUDA / Metal / oneAPI\nset_backend!(ROCBackend())\nsim = run_direct_simulation(params)\nset_backend!(PolyesterBackend())  # back to the CPU\n\nwith_backend(() -> run_direct_simulation(params), ROCBackend())   # the same, scoped\n\nThe grid is constructed on the host and then moved to the device; the time steps (neighbour search, MOOD, boundary conditions, CFL estimate) run there.\nInitial conditions are evaluated on the host and copied over once; each snapshot copies the active particles back. The returned sim therefore holds plain host arrays.\nParticle adaptivity works; a step that actually inserts or merges particles copies the grid to the host and back.\nAll schemes, including :WENO, and all grid movers run on the device (since HyperCloudCore 0.6.1). What still runs on the host is listed under Not yet on the device.\nCustom equations, boundary conditions and source terms run inside the time step and must follow the rules for kernel bodies (bits types, no allocation, no dynamic dispatch). Initial conditions only run on the host.","title":"Running on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#Memory-limit","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"By default the buffers of a run grow on demand; growing a buffer briefly holds the old and the new array at once, which is where a run on a nearly full device fails. set_memory_limit!(bytes) instead sizes every buffer of the following runs once, to the largest particle capacity under the limit (HyperCloudCore's reserve!), and a run that outgrows it stops with a CapacityError:\n\nset_memory_limit!(12 * 2^30; slots_per_particle = 40)   # 12 GiB; room for neighbourhoods to grow\nsim = run_direct_simulation(params)                       # stats: reserved_particles, reserved_bytes\nset_memory_limit!(nothing)\n\nLike the backend it is a session setting, not a parameter, so it does not change the run hash. slots_per_particle defaults to what the initial neighbour lists need; raise it for moving or adaptive grids.\n\nwarning: Device reservations (AMDGPU 2.9.1, ROCm 7.2)\nA reservation on a device can zero grid buffers when earlier device runs happened in the same Julia session (see HyperCloudCore's memory budget notes). The runners compare the grid before and after the reservation and refuse to run when it changed. Until this is fixed upstream, start reserved GPU runs in a fresh session.","title":"Memory limit"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#Direct-simulation","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"run_direct_simulation evolves the PDE given in the PDE namespace.\n\nNo source terms are attached.\nThe initial condition is evaluated at every particle.\nThe stored states are the variables the equation evolves (conservative variables for Euler).","title":"Direct simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#Kinetic-simulation","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"run_kinetic_simulation replaces the macroscopic system with M variables by a linear kinetic system with N_K variables plus a stiff relaxation term (see Kinetic Relaxation).\n\nTwo equations. The macroscopic equation is built first; build_kinetic_system then builds the kinetic LinearAdvection system and the RelaxationSourceTerm from the Kinetic namespace.\nKinetic context. The context is switched to the kinetic equation and M = N_K, so the same schemes run on the kinetic variables. The relaxation term is registered as an implicit source, which requires an IMEX stepper (:IMEXEuler, :ARS222, :ARS233, :PRSSP3, :SSP332).\nInitialization. The macroscopic initial condition is mapped to the kinetic equilibrium (Maxwellian) at every particle.\nOutput. Snapshots are summed back to the macroscopic variables with the source term's Kin2Macro map. Set :save_relax => true to keep the raw kinetic variables instead.","title":"Kinetic simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#BGK-simulation","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"run_bgk_simulation runs the BGK equation (:PDE_name => :bgk). The direct and kinetic runners hand BGK configurations over to it, so the method dictionaries work unchanged.\n\nMacroscopic initial data. The initial condition is built with M = D + 2 (the Euler variables of the same gamma) and every particle starts in the discrete Maxwellian of its state.\nCollision term. BGKRelaxation (implicit) for an IMEX tableau, ExplicitBGKRelaxation for an explicit one, none for :PDE_tau => Inf.\nOutput. Snapshots hold the moments (rho rhovec u E); :save_relax => true keeps the distributions.","title":"BGK simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"simulation/#API","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.run_direct_simulation","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"run_direct_simulation(params::ParamDict) -> Union{AbstractSimData, Nothing}\n\nOrchestrates the standard execution pipeline for explicit macroscopic PDEs.  Extracts configuration namespaces, builds the mathematical equation system, continuous domain, and particle grid, and instantiates the spatial numerical schemes. Applies the initial condition directly to the particle grid and executes the main solver loop, packaging the raw conservative state vectors into an AbstractSimData object. BGK configurations (:PDE_name => :bgk) are handed to run_bgk_simulation, which attaches their collision term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.run_direct_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.run_kinetic_simulation","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"run_kinetic_simulation(params::ParamDict) -> Union{AbstractSimData, Nothing}\n\nManages the execution pipeline for highly coupled kinetic relaxation systems.  Builds a two-tier equation architecture by first constructing the base macroscopic equation, then constructing the kinetic system and its associated RelaxationSourceTerm. Dynamically overwrites the execution context to operate in the higher NK-dimensional space during integration. Automatically collapses the NK-dimensional kinetic data back into the macroscopic state before returning the output, unless save_relax is set to true. BGK configurations (:PDE_name => :bgk), which are kinetic already, are handed to run_bgk_simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.run_kinetic_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.extract_namespace","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"extract_namespace(params::Dict, prefix::Symbol; separator::String=\"_\")\n\nExtracts all keys from a dictionary that start with the given prefix followed by the separator (e.g., PDE_name). Leaves the values exactly as they are (preserving tuples for hashing) and returns a new dictionary with the stripped keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.extract_namespace"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.storage_for","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"storage_for(backend = get_backend()) -> Type\n\nThe array type a run lives on for backend: Array for the CPU backends (PolyesterBackend, ThreadsBackend, KABackend(CPU())), the device array type of a GPU KABackend (e.g. ROCArray).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.storage_for"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.with_backend","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"with_backend(f, backend)\n\nCalls f() with backend as the execution backend and restores the previous backend afterwards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.with_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.set_memory_limit!","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"set_memory_limit!(limit::Integer; tolerance = 0.05, slots_per_particle = nothing)\nset_memory_limit!(nothing)\n\nReserves the buffers of every following run under limit bytes (HyperCloudCore's reserve!): tolerance is the share of the limit kept free for the runtime, slots_per_particle the neighbour slots reserved per particle (default: what the initial neighbour lists need; raise it for moving or adaptive grids). nothing removes the limit (buffers grow on demand, the default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.set_memory_limit!"},{"category":"function","location":"simulation/#HyperCloud.get_memory_limit","page":"Simulation Engine","text":"The memory limit set with set_memory_limit! as (; limit, tolerance, slots_per_particle), or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.get_memory_limit"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Configuration-Reference","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"A simulation is described by one flat Dict{Symbol, Any}. Keys are grouped into namespaces by prefix: :Grid_Ns belongs to the Grid namespace and reaches the builder as :Ns (see extract_namespace). Keys without a prefix are global.\n\nMost namespaces configure numerical components of HyperCloudCore; their builders and defaults are documented in HyperCloudCore's Config-Driven Builders.\n\nA method dictionary (for example from create_method) is merged on top of the shared parameters, so any key below may appear in either place.","title":"Configuration Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Global-keys","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Default Meaning\n:sim_func_name — Pipeline to run: :run_direct_simulation, :run_kinetic_simulation or :run_bgk_simulation. Set automatically by create_method; BGK configurations (:PDE_name => :bgk) run run_bgk_simulation from either of the other two.\n:tmax — Final time. :Time_tmax overrides it.\n:snapshots — Number of output intervals; snapshots + 1 states are stored, including t = 0.\n:remove_ghosts true Drop boundary/ghost particles from the stored snapshots.\n:T Float64 Floating-point type of the simulation.\n:real_type Float64 Floating-point type of the exact reference (analytical_solution).\n:save_relax false Kinetic and BGK runs only: store the raw kinetic variables (distributions) instead of the macroscopic state.","title":"Global keys"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#PDE_-—-equation","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Used by Meaning\n:name all :linear, :burgers, :euler or :bgk. Required.\n:D :burgers, :euler, :bgk Spatial dimension. Linear advection infers D from :velocities.\n:velocities :linear One entry per dimension describing A_d; see Linear Advection.\n:gamma :euler, :bgk Ratio of specific heats (BGK default (D + 2) / D; it fixes the internal degrees of freedom, see BGK).\n:Nv :bgk Velocity nodes per direction (Nv^D discrete velocities).\n:vmax :bgk Half-width of the velocity grid (outermost node for Gauss–Hermite).\n:velocity_grid :bgk :uniform (default) or :gauss_hermite.\n:vcenter :bgk Centre of the velocity grid per direction (default 0).\n:tau :bgk Constant relaxation time; Inf is collisionless.\n:viscosity, :omega :bgk Instead of :tau: τ = μ / p with μ = viscosity * θ^omega (omega default 0.5).\n\nThe Euler representation is read from the same namespace by parse_representation: :representation is :conservative (default), :primitive or :lagrangian, the latter two with an integration :path (default :mapped).","title":"PDE_ — equation"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Grid_-—-domain-and-particles","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Default Meaning\n:domain — :rectangular, :spherical, or a pre-built GeometricDomain. Required.\n:mins, :maxs — Bounding box (tuples of length D). A spherical domain uses their midpoint as center and half the first extent as radius.\n:periodic false Bool or NTuple{D, Bool}.\n:bc none Boundary conditions: Dict(tag => bc) where bc is an AbstractBoundaryCondition, :outflow, :fixed_dirichlet, or the name of a zero-argument constructor (looked up in HyperCloudCore, then in Main; see parse_bc). A string is parsed as Julia code.\n:Ns — Particles per direction (tuple). The nominal spacing is (maxs - mins) ./ Ns.\n:randomness_factor — Jitter per direction as a fraction of the spacing (tuple).\n:seed 42 RNG seed for the jitter.\n:mover :none Grid motion: :none, :lagrangian, :scaled_lagrangian (factor :mover_alpha) or :sinusoidal (amplitude :mover_amplitude); see Moving Grids. Lagrangian movers need fluid_velocity for the equation; all built-in equations define it.\n:volumes :shepard Particle volume estimator: :shepard (:volumes_sweeps) or :mls (:volumes_degree, :volumes_samples_per_dx); see Particle Volumes. Used for the initial volumes and, on a moving grid, for the re-estimation after every step and every adaptation call (HyperCloudCore ≥ 0.7.5; before, only the initial volumes). On a moving grid Shepard volumes give a resolution-independent bias in the discrete mass; :mls converges but costs about 3 (1-D) to 20 (2-D) time steps per re-estimation.\n\nwarning: The key is `:Grid_bc`\nBoundary conditions are read from :Grid_bc. A key such as :Grid_bc_map is silently ignored.","title":"Grid_ — domain and particles"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Weight_-—-MLS-weight-function","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Meaning\n:name :exponential or :inverse. Required.\n:range Support radius in multiples of the largest grid spacing. Required. High reconstruction orders need a larger range (the examples use 5.5).\n:alpha Shape parameter of the weight. Required.","title":"Weight_ — MLS weight function"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#IC_-—-initial-condition","page":"Configuration Reference","text":":name selects the initial condition; every other key is converted to the simulation type T.\n\n:name Type Keys\n:gauss Gauss :a (amplitude, length M), :b (center, length D), :width\n:box Box :u_bg, :u_box (length M), :mins, :maxs (length D)\n:sine Sine :a, :c_offset (length M), :period (length D)\n:riemann Riemann :uL, :uR (length M), :p0 (length D), :n (optional, default +x, normalized)\n:s_riemann SRiemann as :riemann, plus :width\n:q_riemann QRiemann :states (tuple of 2^D states, binary ordering), :p0\n\nStates are given in the variables the equation evolves. For Euler these are the conservative variables (rho rhovec u E).","title":"IC_ — initial condition"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Scheme_-—-spatial-discretization","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Default Meaning\n:name — :MUSCL, :Upwind, :Central or :WENO. Required.\n:order — Consistency order (expected convergence rate; MUSCL reconstructs with degree order - 1). :Upwind only supports 1, :WENO needs at least 2.\n:MLS_order 0 Fixed order of the MLS divergence operator; 0 follows each particle's current order.\n:upwind_alg_nd — :Upwind only: :Classic (any system), :Tiwari (scalar) or :Praveen (scalar, 2D).\n\nThe schemes are described in HyperCloudCore: MUSCL, Upwind, Central and WENO (scalar equations only).","title":"Scheme_ — spatial discretization"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Flux_,-Limiter_,-MOOD_","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Default Meaning\n:Flux_name — :Rusanov or :Upwind. Required.\n:Limiter_name :none :none, :minmod, :superbee, :VK (Venkatakrishnan), :BJ (Barth–Jespersen).\n:Limiter_mode — Required by every active limiter, e.g. :hard.\n:MOOD_criterion :none :none, :only, :U1, :U2.\n:MOOD_strategy :none :EPD0, :SEPD0, :EPD1, :EPD2. Required when a criterion is set.\n:MOOD_delta_relax — Required by :U1/:U2. Multiplied by the cell volume prod(dx).\n:MOOD_plateau 1e-3 :U2 only (HyperCloudCore ≥ 0.7.6): a component that varies across the stencil by less than this fraction of its global range is not checked. Keeps smooth far fields (e.g. a vortex tail) at full order; 0 turns it off.\n:MOOD_curvature_ratio 0.5 :U2 only (HyperCloudCore ≥ 0.7.6): Diot's curvature-magnitude test, applied among the nearest neighbours; 0 turns it off.\n\nSee HyperCloudCore's Numerical Flux Functions, the limiters and MOOD for what the options do.","title":"Flux_, Limiter_, MOOD_"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Time_-—-time-stepping","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Key Meaning\n:stepper Explicit: :Euler, :RK2 (SSP), :RK2Ralston, :RK3 (SSP), :RK4SSP (SSPRK(5,4), Spiteri–Ruuth), :RK4 (classical). IMEX: :IMEXEuler, :ARS222, :ARS233, :PRSSP3, :SSP332.\n:CFL CFL number; the step is recomputed every iteration. It is not a classical Courant number, see what the CFL number means on a point cloud.\n:dt Fixed time step, used when :CFL is absent.\n:tmax Optional override of the global :tmax.\n:reset_orders_per_stage true restores the maximum order before every stage (default false: orders lowered by MOOD stay lowered for the step).\n:full_search_per_stage true rebuilds the neighbour lists at every stage of a moving grid (default false).\n:mood_substeps true (default, HyperCloudCore ≥ 0.7.7): with an explicit SSP tableau MOOD checks the forward Euler substeps Y + (Δt/C) K of the canonical Shu–Osher form instead of the stage values, so the admissibility of every substep carries over to every stage and the result (also for :RK4SSP, whose final update reuses earlier substeps). false checks the stage values.\n\nIMEX steppers treat the source terms implicitly and are used with the kinetic pipeline. See HyperCloudCore's Butcher Tableaus for the properties of each tableau.","title":"Time_ — time stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Adapt_-—-particle-insertion-and-merging","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Optional; mainly for Lagrangian grids. See Particle insertion and merging.\n\nKey Default Meaning\n:enabled false Insert and merge particles between time steps.\n:max_gap, :min_gap 1.6, 0.5 Pair distances (in units of the spacing) above which a particle is inserted and below which a pair is merged.\n:clearance 0.7 Free radius required around an insertion point.\n:samples_per_dx 2 Lattice resolution of the conservative remap.\n:exclude_reduced_order true No events near particles whose MOOD order was lowered.\n:max_events unlimited At most this many insertions/merges per call (HyperCloudCore ≥ 0.7.5).\n\nEvery adaptation call is exactly conservative with respect to Σ V_i U_i, also with several events per call and, on a moving grid, across the volume re-estimation that follows (HyperCloudCore ≥ 0.7.5; with 0.7.4 and earlier, calls with several events in 2-D/3-D and the next re-estimation were not, see Particle insertion and merging).\n\nThe number of particles at the end is stored in the statistic final_particle_count.","title":"Adapt_ — particle insertion and merging"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Kinetic_-—-relaxation-system","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"Only read by run_kinetic_simulation.\n\nKey Default Meaning\n:velocities — Kinetic speeds, one NK-tuple per dimension. Use generate_relax_params.\n:indices — Vector{Int} of edges mapping kinetic to macroscopic variables (see Kin2Macro).\n:epsilon — Relaxation time epsilon.\n:interior_factor D Scaling of the inverse kinetic speeds in the Maxwellian.\n:save_relax false Same as the global key.","title":"Kinetic_ — relaxation system"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Complete-example","page":"Configuration Reference","text":"using HyperCloud, StaticArrays\n\nvels, idx = generate_relax_params(2.0, 1, 1)\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}(\n    :sim_func_name => :run_kinetic_simulation,\n    :tmax => 0.5, :snapshots => 10,\n\n    :PDE_name => :burgers, :PDE_D => 1,\n\n    :Grid_domain => :rectangular, :Grid_mins => (-1.0,), :Grid_maxs => (1.0,),\n    :Grid_periodic => true, :Grid_Ns => (200,),\n    :Grid_randomness_factor => (0.2,), :Grid_seed => 10,\n\n    :Weight_name => :exponential, :Weight_alpha => 1.0, :Weight_range => 5.5,\n\n    :IC_name => :gauss, :IC_a => (1.0,), :IC_b => (0.0,), :IC_width => 0.5,\n\n    :Scheme_name => :MUSCL, :Scheme_order => 2,\n    :Flux_name => :Rusanov, :Limiter_name => :none,\n    :MOOD_criterion => :U2, :MOOD_strategy => :EPD1, :MOOD_delta_relax => 1e-4,\n\n    :Time_stepper => :ARS222, :Time_CFL => 0.2,\n\n    :Kinetic_velocities => vels, :Kinetic_indices => idx, :Kinetic_epsilon => 1e-6,\n)\n\nsim = run_kinetic_simulation(params)   # `nothing` if anything failed (the error is logged)\nexact = analytical_solution(params)\nexact(SVector(0.3, 0.5))               # exact state at x = 0.3, t = 0.5","title":"Complete example"},{"category":"section","location":"wrappers/#Method-Wrappers","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"Writing method dictionaries by hand is tedious and error-prone. These helpers create them with consistent keys and labels.","title":"Method Wrappers"},{"category":"section","location":"wrappers/#create_method","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"label, method = create_method(:RK2, :MUSCL, 2; limiter = :VK)\n# label  == \"RK2MUSCL2(VK)\"\n# method == Dict(:Time_stepper => :RK2, :Scheme_name => :MUSCL, :Scheme_order => 2,\n#                :Limiter_name => :VK, :Limiter_mode => :hard, ...)\n\nReturns label => method, where method uses the namespaced keys of the Configuration Reference.\n\nPipeline. Unless sim_func_name is given, IMEX steppers (:IMEXEuler, :ARS222, :ARS233, :PRSSP3, :SSP332) select :run_kinetic_simulation and all others :run_direct_simulation.\nLabel. \"<stepper><scheme><order>\", followed in parentheses by any non-default flux, the limiter, and for MOOD the strategy (plus the criterion if it is not :U2). Examples: \"RK2MUSCL2\", \"RK2MUSCL2(VK)\", \"RK2MUSCL2(EPD1)\", \"RK2MUSCL2(EPD2,U1)\", \"RK4MUSCL3(Upwind,minmod)\". custom_label replaces it.\nExtra keywords are stored as additional keys, e.g. Scheme_upwind_alg_nd = :Classic or MOOD_delta_relax = 1e-4. String values are converted to Symbols.\nignore lists parameters the method should ignore. The built-in direct methods pass the kinetic keys (ignore_relax).\n\nThe :Upwind scheme only exists at order 1.","title":"create_method"},{"category":"section","location":"wrappers/#get_master_method_dict","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"Returns all built-in methods as Dict(label => method):\n\nfirst-order baselines: EulerUpwind1, RK2Upwind1, IMEXEulerUpwind1, ARS222Upwind1, ARS233Upwind1, and RK2WENO2;\ndirect second order: RK2MUSCL2 without limiter, with VK, BJ or minmod, and with MOOD (EPD0, EPD1, EPD2, SEPD0, U1), plus RK2MUSCL1;\nkinetic second order: ARS222MUSCL2 without limiter, with VK or minmod, and with MOOD (EPD1, EPD2, SEPD0, U1);\nhigh order: RK4MUSCL2–RK4MUSCL5 without limiter, with VK, minmod, or MOOD (EPD1, EPD2), RK4MUSCL4(EPD1,only), and kinetic ARS233MUSCL2–ARS233MUSCL5 with MOOD.\n\nSelect methods by label in a SimulationConfig, e.g. [\"RK2MUSCL2\", \"ARS222MUSCL2\"].","title":"get_master_method_dict"},{"category":"section","location":"wrappers/#generate_relax_params","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"vels, idx = generate_relax_params(2.0, 2, 1)\n# vels == ((2.0, -2.0, 0.0, 0.0), (0.0, 0.0, 2.0, -2.0))\n# idx  == [1, 5]\n\nBuilds the standard kinetic velocity set: 2D kinetic variables per macroscopic variable, with speeds pm v along each axis. It returns the speeds in the format expected by :Kinetic_velocities and the edges for :Kinetic_indices. See Kinetic Relaxation.","title":"generate_relax_params"},{"category":"section","location":"wrappers/#API","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"wrappers/#HyperCloud.create_method","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"create_method(timestepper::Symbol, main_gradient::Symbol, order::Int; kwargs...)\n\nAutomatically generates a method ParamDict using the strict namespace API,  automatically assigns the correct simulation pipeline, and generates a standardized plot label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.create_method"},{"category":"function","location":"wrappers/#HyperCloud.get_master_method_dict","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"get_master_method_dict()\n\nReturns a MethodDict containing every standard numerical scheme in the Meshfree framework.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.get_master_method_dict"},{"category":"function","location":"wrappers/#HyperCloud.generate_relax_params","page":"Convenience Wrappers","text":"generate_relax_params(v_mag::Float64, dim::Int, NM::Int)\n\nGenerates symmetric kinetic velocities matching the native Flux{D, NK} tuple format  along with the uniformly partitioned edge indices (relax_indices) for the kinetic system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.generate_relax_params"},{"category":"section","location":"ICs/#Initial-Conditions","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"An initial condition is a callable struct that subtypes InitialCondition. It takes a position pos::Space{D} and returns the state State{M} at that point.\n\nset_initial_conditions! evaluates it at every particle. The kinetic pipeline uses a second method that maps the macroscopic state to the kinetic equilibrium (see Kinetic Relaxation).\n\nIn a configuration the initial condition is selected with :IC_name; build_ic converts all IC_ parameters to the simulation type T. The keys of each type are listed in the Configuration Reference.","title":"Initial Conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"ICs/#Available-initial-conditions","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Type :IC_name State at pos\nGauss :gauss a expleft(-lVert pos - b rVert^2  mathrmwidth^2right)\nBox :box u_box if mins .<= pos .<= maxs in every direction, else u_bg\nSine :sine a sinleft(2pi sum_d pos_d  mathrmperiod_dright) + c_mathrmoffset\nRiemann :riemann uL if (pos - p_0) cdot n  0, else uR\nSRiemann :s_riemann fracu_L + u_R2 - fracu_L - u_Rpi arctanleft(frac(pos - p_0)cdot nmathrmwidthright)\nQRiemann :q_riemann u_states[1 + Σ_d 2^(d-1) (pos_d ≥ p0_d)]\n\nNotes:\n\nRiemann normals. build_ic normalizes n (default +x). uL lies on the side the normal points away from.\nQuadrant ordering. QRiemann needs 2^D states in binary order. In 2D: (bottom-left, bottom-right, top-left, top-right).\nPrecision. Gauss and SRiemann store their parameters in the precision of the amplitude/state. A Float32 configuration therefore evaluates entirely in Float32.","title":"Available initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"ICs/#Adding-an-initial-condition","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Define a struct, make it callable, and add a builder method:\n\nusing HyperCloud, StaticArrays\n\nstruct Ring{D, M, T} <: InitialCondition\n    u_in::State{M, T}\n    u_out::State{M, T}\n    center::Space{D, T}\n    radius::T\nend\n\n(ic::Ring)(pos::Space{D}) where {D} = sum(abs2, pos - ic.center) < ic.radius^2 ? ic.u_in : ic.u_out\n\nfunction HyperCloud.build_ic(::Val{:ring}, conf::Dict, ctx::Dict)\n    T, D, M = ctx[:T]::DataType, ctx[:D]::Int, ctx[:M]::Int\n    return Ring(State{M, T}(Tuple(T.(conf[:u_in]))), State{M, T}(Tuple(T.(conf[:u_out]))),\n                Space{D, T}(Tuple(T.(conf[:center]))), T(conf[:radius]))\nend\n\n:IC_name => :ring now selects it. To use it as a reference solution, add an analytic_closure method for the equations where it has one (see PDE Architecture).","title":"Adding an initial condition"},{"category":"section","location":"ICs/#API","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.InitialCondition","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"InitialCondition\n\nAbstract base type for all initial condition functors in the framework. Functors derived from this type evaluate the starting state of the physical system at a given spatial coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.InitialCondition"},{"category":"function","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.set_initial_conditions!","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"set_initial_conditions!(pg::ParticleGrid, eq::HyperbolicPDE, IC::InitialCondition)\nset_initial_conditions!(pg::ParticleGrid, st::RelaxationSourceTerm, IC::InitialCondition, eq_macro::HyperbolicPDE)\n\nEvaluates the initial condition functor and applies it to the particle grid.\n\nDirect formulation: evaluates IC at every particle position and assigns the physical state to pg.rhos.\nKinetic formulation: evaluates the macroscopic state and physical fluxes at every particle and initializes the kinetic variables directly in the Maxwellian equilibrium, component by component.\n\n\n\n\n\nset_initial_conditions!(pg::ParticleGrid, eq::BGKEquation, IC::InitialCondition)\n\nFor the BGK equation IC gives the macroscopic state (ρ, ρu..., E) (the conservative Euler variables of euler_limit); every particle starts in the conservative discrete Maxwellian of that state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.set_initial_conditions!"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.Box","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Box{D, M, T} <: InitialCondition\n\nDefines a discontinuous rectangular sub-region.\n\nFields\n\nu_bg::State{M, T}: The background state applied outside the box.\nu_box::State{M, T}: The interior state applied within the box.\nmins::Space{D, T}: The lower spatial bounds of the rectangular region.\nmaxs::Space{D, T}: The upper spatial bounds of the rectangular region.\n\nEvaluates to u_box if mins .<= pos .<= maxs holds in every direction (the boundary belongs to the box), and to u_bg otherwise. Mixed-precision inputs are promoted to one T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.Box"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.Gauss","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Gauss{D, M, T} <: InitialCondition\n\nEvaluates a smooth, multi-dimensional Gaussian pulse.\n\nFields\n\na::State{M, T}: The amplitude state vector.\nb::Space{D, T}: The spatial center coordinate of the pulse.\nwidth::T: The parameter controlling the spatial spread of the bell curve.\n\nThe physical state is computed as a * exp(-sum(abs2, pos - b) / width^2). The center and width are converted to the precision T of the amplitude, so a Float32 amplitude gives a pure Float32 pulse even if b or width are Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.Gauss"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.QRiemann","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"QRiemann{D, M, N_states, T} <: InitialCondition\n\nDefines a multi-dimensional quadrant Riemann problem that partitions the domain into orthogonal regions intersecting at a central point.\n\nFields\n\nu_states::NTuple{N_states, State{M, T}}: A tuple of constant physical states corresponding to each spatial quadrant.\np0::Space{D, T}: The central intersection point of the quadrants.\n\nThe state index is 1 + Σ_d 2^(d-1) * (pos[d] >= p0[d]), so u_states must hold 2^D states ordered by binary encoding. In 2D: (bottom-left, bottom-right, top-left, top-right).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.QRiemann"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.Riemann","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Riemann{D, M, T} <: InitialCondition\n\nDefines a standard discontinuous Riemann problem (e.g., a shock tube or half-space) split by a hyperplane.\n\nFields\n\nuL::State{M, T}: The physical state vector on the \"left\" side of the hyperplane.\nuR::State{M, T}: The physical state vector on the \"right\" side of the hyperplane.\np0::Space{D, T}: An origin point located exactly on the separating hyperplane.\nn::Space{D, T}: The normal vector of the hyperplane pointing toward the right state.\n\nReturns uL if the dot product of the relative position and the normal is negative, and uR otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.Riemann"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.Sine","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"Sine{D, M, T} <: InitialCondition\n\nEvaluates a smooth, multi-dimensional periodic sinusoidal wave.\n\nFields\n\na::State{M, T}: The amplitude state of the wave.\nperiod::Space{D, T}: The spatial periodicity along each axis.\nc_offset::State{M, T}: The constant background offset state.\n\nThe physical state is a * sin(2π * sum(pos ./ period)) + c_offset. It is evaluated with sinpi, which keeps the input precision (no promotion to Float64 via pi) and is exact at integer multiples of the period.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.Sine"},{"category":"type","location":"ICs/#HyperCloud.SRiemann","page":"Initial Conditions","text":"SRiemann{D, M, T} <: InitialCondition\n\nSmoothed, continuous approximation of a standard Riemann problem that avoids infinite initial gradients at the interface.\n\nFields\n\nuL::State{M, T}: The asymptotic physical state on the \"left\".\nuR::State{M, T}: The asymptotic physical state on the \"right\".\np0::Space{D, T}: A point located on the central interface.\nn::Space{D, T}: The normal vector pointing toward the right state.\nwidth::T: Controls the steepness of the transition.\n\nUses an arctangent to blend smoothly between uL and uR over the given width. All fields are converted to the precision T of uL.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.SRiemann"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Burgers'-Equation","page":"Burgers","text":"BurgersEquation{D, T} implements the inviscid Burgers' equation in D dimensions:\n\nfracpartial upartial t + sum_d=1^D fracpartialpartial x_dleft(fracu^22right) = 0 \n\nThe state is a scalar (M = 1). Information travels with velocity (u dots u), so compressive regions steepen into shocks and expansive regions open into rarefaction fans.\n\nIn a configuration: :PDE_name => :burgers, :PDE_D => D.","title":"Burgers' Equation"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Implementation","page":"Burgers","text":"Variables. prim2cons and cons2prim are the identity.\nFlux. F_d(u) = u^22 in every direction.\nWave speed. max_eigenvalue returns u; velocity returns the 1 times 1 Jacobian u.\nMoving grids. fluid_velocity returns (u dots u), the characteristic velocity, so a Lagrangian grid follows the characteristics.","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Exact-solutions","page":"Burgers","text":"","title":"Exact solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Riemann-and-smoothed-Riemann-(any-dimension)","page":"Burgers","text":"Along the normal coordinate xi = (x - p_0)cdot n the equation reduces to a 1D law with flux au^22, where a = sum_d n_d. The characteristic speeds are a u_L and a u_R:\n\na u_L  a u_R: a shock moving at a (u_L + u_R)2 (Rankine–Hugoniot).\notherwise: a rarefaction fan with u = xi  (a t) between the two speeds.\n\nThe wave type depends on the sign of a, so flipping the normal and swapping the states gives the same solution. The normal does not need to be normalized. For SRiemann the closure returns the sharp Riemann solution, a good reference once the waves are wider than width.","title":"Riemann and smoothed Riemann (any dimension)"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Gauss-(any-dimension)","page":"Burgers","text":"The solution is given implicitly by u = u_0(x - utmathbf1), which is solved with Newton's method. This is only valid before the pulse breaks, at\n\nt^* = fracmathrmwidthasqrtfrace2D \n\nPast t^* Newton may not converge; the closure then warns and returns its last iterate.","title":"Gauss (any dimension)"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Box-(1D)","page":"Burgers","text":"The top-hat case (u_box > u_bg) produces a rarefaction at the left edge and a shock at the right edge. The well case (u_box < u_bg) produces a shock at the left edge and a rarefaction at the right edge. Both are exact before and after the time the fan catches up with the shock, t_mathrmint = 2(x_e - x_s)u_mathrmbox - u_mathrmbg. After that the shock moves like sqrtt relative to the background flow and its strength decays like t^-12.","title":"Box (1D)"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#Other-initial-conditions","page":"Burgers","text":"There is no exact solution. The closure warns and returns the initial condition, which is only correct at t = 0.","title":"Other initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/burgers/#API","page":"Burgers","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/burgers/#HyperCloud.BurgersEquation","page":"Burgers","text":"BurgersEquation{D, T} <: HyperbolicPDE{D, 1, T}\n\nImplements the multi-dimensional inviscid Burgers' equation.  The system mathematically enforces a strictly scalar state vector (M = 1) across any spatial dimension D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.BurgersEquation"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Builder-API","page":"Builder API","text":"The runners never construct solver components directly. They split the parameter dictionary into namespaces with extract_namespace and call one build_* function per component. Every builder dispatches on a Val of a configuration value, so new components are added by defining a method, without changing HyperCloud.\n\nThe builders come from two packages:\n\nPhysics, in HyperCloud: build_equation, build_ic and build_kinetic_system (documented below).\nNumerics, in HyperCloudCore: domain, particle grid, weights, flux, limiter, MOOD, scheme, tableau, time stepper and adaptivity. They are re-exported by HyperCloud and documented in HyperCloudCore's Config-Driven Builders, together with the context keys they read and write.","title":"Builder API"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Build-order-and-context","page":"Builder API","text":"The builders share a context::Dict{Symbol, Any}. Each one reads what earlier builders stored and adds its own result. run_direct_simulation runs them in this order:\n\nStep Builder Reads from context Stores\n0 runner — :T, :Storage (storage_for), :MemoryLimit (only with set_memory_limit!)\n1 build_equation :T runner stores :Equation, :D, :M, :ExplicitSources, :ImplicitSources\n2 build_domain :T, :D runner stores :Domain, :WeightConf\n3 build_particle_grid :T, :D, :M, :Domain, :WeightConf, :Storage :max_dx, :Grid\n3a build_weights, build_mover, build_volume_estimator (called by step 3) :T, :max_dx, :Domain :interp_range\n3b build_adaptivity :T —\n4 build_flux, build_limiter, build_mood :T, :Grid (MOOD) runner stores :Flux, :Limiter, :MOOD\n5 build_scheme :T, :D, :M, :Flux, :Limiter, :Storage runner stores :Scheme\n6 build_tableau, build_timestepper everything above runner stores :Tableau; :Memory (with a memory limit)\n7 build_ic :T, :D, :M (macroscopic) —\n\nThe particle grid is built on a CPU backend and then moved to the device when :Storage is a device array type, and the time stepper's memory reservation is checked against a fingerprint of the grid (see Running on a GPU).\n\nrun_kinetic_simulation builds the initial condition right after step 1, while :M still holds the macroscopic size. Then build_kinetic_system overwrites :Equation and :M with the kinetic system and puts the relaxation term into :ImplicitSources, and the remaining steps run on the kinetic variables.\n\n:T is the floating-point type (params[:T], default Float64). The runners set :ExplicitSources and :ImplicitSources to () unless a source term is added.","title":"Build order and context"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Extending-the-builders","page":"Builder API","text":"Builder Package Dispatches on Extend with\nbuild_equation HyperCloud :PDE_name HyperCloud.build_equation(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_ic HyperCloud :IC_name HyperCloud.build_ic(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_domain HyperCloudCore :Grid_domain HyperCloudCore.build_domain(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_weights HyperCloudCore :Weight_name HyperCloudCore.build_weights(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_mover HyperCloudCore :Grid_mover HyperCloudCore.build_mover(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_volume_estimator HyperCloudCore :Grid_volumes HyperCloudCore.build_volume_estimator(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_flux HyperCloudCore :Flux_name HyperCloudCore.build_flux(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_limiter HyperCloudCore :Limiter_name HyperCloudCore.build_limiter(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_mood_criterion HyperCloudCore :MOOD_criterion HyperCloudCore.build_mood_criterion(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_mood_strategy HyperCloudCore :MOOD_strategy HyperCloudCore.build_mood_strategy(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_scheme HyperCloudCore :Scheme_name HyperCloudCore.build_scheme(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\nbuild_tableau HyperCloudCore :Time_stepper HyperCloudCore.build_tableau(::Val{:x}, conf, ctx)\n\nBecause HyperCloud re-exports HyperCloudCore, HyperCloud.build_scheme and HyperCloudCore.build_scheme are the same function; qualifying with the owning package makes it clear where the method goes. Unknown names raise an error naming the missing method. A complete example for initial conditions is in Initial Conditions; a new equation is described in PDE Architecture.\n\nA custom spatial scheme follows the same pattern. The returned object must be a DivergenceInterpolator that implements HyperCloudCore's scheme contract:\n\nfunction HyperCloudCore.build_scheme(::Val{:my_scheme}, conf::Dict, context::Dict)\n    T = context[:T]::DataType\n    D = context[:D]::Int\n    M = context[:M]::Int\n    return MyScheme(T, D, M, conf[:my_param]; flux = context[:Flux])\nend\n\n:Scheme_name => :my_scheme, :Scheme_my_param => 3 then selects it.","title":"Extending the builders"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Custom-boundary-conditions","page":"Builder API","text":":Grid_bc maps boundary tags to boundary conditions (parse_bc). Besides instances and the names :outflow and :fixed_dirichlet, it accepts the name of any zero-argument constructor defined in HyperCloudCore or in Main:\n\nstruct MyWall <: AbstractBoundaryCondition end   # plus the functor HyperCloudCore requires\nparams[:Grid_bc] = Dict(1 => :MyWall, 2 => :outflow)\n\nThe functor a boundary condition implements is described in HyperCloudCore's Boundary Conditions.","title":"Custom boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#API","page":"Builder API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"builder/#HyperCloud.build_equation","page":"Builder API","text":"build_equation(pde_conf::Dict, context::Dict)\n\nConstructs the physical equation system based on the provided configuration dictionary. Strictly requires a :name Symbol (e.g., :linear, :burgers, :euler) in the configuration to dynamically dispatch to the correct specific constructor using Val.\n\n\n\n\n\nbuild_equation(eq_name::Val, pde_conf::Dict, context::Dict)\n\nConstructs the physical equation system based on the provided configuration dictionary. Strictly requires a :name Symbol (e.g., :linear, :burgers, :euler) in the configuration to dynamically dispatch to the correct specific constructor using Val.\n\n\n\n\n\nbuild_equation(::Val{:bgk}, pde_conf::Dict, context::Dict) -> BGKEquation\n\nDiscrete-velocity BGK model. Keys of the PDE namespace:\n\nKey Meaning\n:D space dimension (the velocity grid has the same dimension)\n:Nv velocity nodes per direction (Nv^D discrete velocities)\n:vmax half-width of the velocity grid (outermost node for :gauss_hermite)\n:velocity_grid :uniform (default) or :gauss_hermite, see velocity_grid\n:vcenter centre of the velocity grid per direction (default 0)\n:gamma ratio of specific heats (default (D + 2) / D, no internal degrees of freedom)\n:tau constant relaxation time (ConstantRelaxationTime); Inf is collisionless\n:viscosity, :omega instead of :tau: τ = μ / p with μ = viscosity θ^omega (ViscosityRelaxationTime, omega defaults to 0.5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.build_equation"},{"category":"function","location":"builder/#HyperCloud.build_ic","page":"Builder API","text":"build_ic(ic_conf::Dict, context::Dict)\n\nReads the :IC namespace and dispatches to the matching builder via Val(ic_conf[:name]). Extend it by defining build_ic(::Val{:my_ic}, conf::Dict, ctx::Dict).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.build_ic"},{"category":"function","location":"builder/#HyperCloud.build_kinetic_system","page":"Builder API","text":"build_kinetic_system(kin_conf::Dict, context::Dict)\n\nConstructs the kinetic equation and relaxation source term for kinetic simulations. Requires a :velocities key in the kinetic configuration dictionary. Extracts the relaxation parameter (epsilon), edge indices (indices), and interior_factor to build a Kin2Macro mapping, a LinearAdvection kinetic equation, and a RelaxationSourceTerm. Returns the tuple (eq_kin, source_term).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.build_kinetic_system"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"HyperCloud ships with benchmark configurations covering the built-in equations and the simulation pipelines.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Running-an-example","page":"Examples","text":"Pass the example function, uncalled, to a macro:\n\nusing HyperCloud\nusing PDEStudio\nusing GLMakie          # or WGLMakie for browser-based rendering\n\n@plot euler_1d_sod     # run (or load from cache) and open the viewer\n\nWithout a viewer, for example on a cluster:\n\nusing HyperCloud\n\n@sim euler_1d_sod","title":"Running an example"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Available-benchmarks","page":"Examples","text":"","title":"Available benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#1D-Sod-shock-tube-(euler_1d_sod)","page":"Examples","text":"The classic Riemann problem for the Euler equations with gamma = 14.\n\nData: (rho u p) = (1 0 1) for x  0 and (0125 0 01) for x  0, given to the IC as conservative states. SOD_LEFT and SOD_RIGHT hold them.\nGrid: 330 jittered particles on -05 05, fixed Dirichlet on the left and outflow on the right, tmax = 0.2.\nMethods: the exact solution, first-order RK2Upwind1, RK2MUSCL2_VK (Venkatakrishnan limiter), and MUSCL with MOOD (RK2MUSCL2_U2_EPD1, RK4MUSCL4_U2_EPD1, plus the inactive RK2MUSCL2_U2_EPD2 and RK2MUSCL2_U1_EPD1).","title":"1D Sod shock tube (euler_1d_sod)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#1D-kinetic-Burgers-(burgers_kinetic)","page":"Examples","text":"Compares a direct scheme with the kinetic relaxation formulation for Burgers' equation.\n\nData: a Gaussian pulse (a = 1, width = 1) on the periodic domain -5 5 with 300 particles, up to tmax = 1.0. The pulse breaks at t^* approx 117, so the run stays in the smooth regime where the exact solution is available.\nKinetic setup: speeds pm 2 from generate_relax_params(2.0, 1, 1) and epsilon = 10^-6.\nMethods: RK2MUSCL2 (direct) and ARS222MUSCL2 (kinetic, IMEX).\nSweep: grid seeds 10, 100, 1000, 10000.","title":"1D kinetic Burgers (burgers_kinetic)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#1D-BGK-shock-tube-(bgk_1d_sod)","page":"Examples","text":"The Sod problem for the discrete-velocity BGK model with gamma = 14.\n\nModel: 24 uniform velocity nodes on -6 6 and the Chu-reduced pair (f h) (48 equations); the IC gives the Sod states as Euler variables.\nGrid: as euler_1d_sod (330 jittered particles, Dirichlet left, outflow right, tmax = 0.2).\nMethods: ARS222MUSCL2_VK and ARS222MUSCL2_U2_EPD1 (implicit collision), the inactive explicit RK2MUSCL2_VK (only stable for the larger tau), against the exact Euler solution (reference = :euler_limit_solution).\nSweep: relaxation times tau = 10^-5 10^-3 10^-2: the hydrodynamic limit, then increasingly viscous and heat-conducting profiles.","title":"1D BGK shock tube (bgk_1d_sod)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#2D-linear-advection-(advection_2d)","page":"Examples","text":"Transport of a Gaussian pulse along the diagonal.\n\nData: velocity (1 1) on the periodic square -5 5^2 with 50 times 50 jittered particles, up to tmax = 10 (one full period in each direction).\nMethods: RK2MUSCL2 against the exact solution from characteristic tracing.\nSweep: grid seeds 10, 100, 1000, 10000.","title":"2D linear advection (advection_2d)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Writing-your-own","page":"Examples","text":"Copy one of the example functions, change the parameters, and run it with @sim or @plot. The Configuration Reference lists every key. Saving the function as my_case.jl in a folder listed in CONFIG_SEARCH_PATHS lets you call @sim my_case from any session without including the file first.","title":"Writing your own"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/euler/#Euler-Equations","page":"Euler","text":"EulerEquation{D, M, T, R} implements the compressible Euler equations for an ideal gas in D dimensions, with conservative variables U = (rho rhovec u E) and M = D + 2:\n\nbeginaligned\npartial_t rho + nablacdot(rhovec u) = 0\npartial_t (rhovec u) + nablacdot(rhovec uotimesvec u + p I) = 0\npartial_t E + nablacdotbig(vec u(E + p)big) = 0\nendaligned\nqquad\nE = fracpgamma - 1 + frac12rholVertvec urVert^2 \n\nIn a configuration: :PDE_name => :euler, :PDE_D => D, :PDE_gamma => 1.4. Initial states are given as conservative variables.","title":"Euler Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/euler/#Implementation","page":"Euler","text":"Construction. EulerEquation(Val(D), T, gamma, rep) sets M = D + 2. rep is the representation tag (default Conservative()).\nVariables. prim2cons maps (rho vec u p) mapsto (rho rhovec u E). cons2prim is its inverse and floors density and pressure at 10^-7.\nFlux. Defined for the Conservative representation.\nWave speed. max_eigenvalue returns u_d + c with c = sqrtgamma p  rho, using a 10^-9 floor on density and pressure. For Conservative it expects conservative variables; for an NCRepresentation it expects primitive variables.\nAdmissibility. is_admissible accepts finite conservative states with positive density and pressure. MOOD flags every candidate that fails it, whatever its criterion says (HyperCloudCore's PAD).\nMoving grids. fluid_velocity returns vec u, so the Lagrangian movers work.","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/euler/#Exact-solution:-1D-Riemann-problem","page":"Euler","text":"For Riemann initial data in 1D the closure computes the exact solution (e.g. for the Sod shock tube):\n\nStar pressure. p^* is found with Newton's method on the pressure function, using a finite-difference derivative and the tolerance f(p^*)  10^-9. This gives u^*.\nWave types. Each outer wave is a shock if p^*  p_LR, otherwise a rarefaction.\nEvaluation. At a point (x t) the closure selects the region by the similarity variable (x - x_0)t: outer state, rarefaction fan, star state left or right of the contact. It returns conservative variables.\n\nIf the normal points in -x, the problem is solved in the mirrored frame, so both orientations of the same data give the same solution. Vacuum generation is not handled: p^* is floored at 10^-9.","title":"Exact solution: 1D Riemann problem"},{"category":"section","location":"PDEs/euler/#API","page":"Euler","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"PDEs/euler/#HyperCloud.EulerEquation","page":"Euler","text":"EulerEquation{D, M, T, R} <: HyperbolicPDE{D, M, T}\n\nImplements the compressible Euler equations for ideal gases. \n\nFields\n\ngamma::T: The specific heat ratio of the gas.\nrep::R: The equation representation (e.g., Conservative or NCRepresentation).\n\nConstructors\n\nEulerEquation(::Val{D}, ::Type{T}, gamma::T = T(1.4), rep::R = Conservative()): Automatically configures the system for spatial dimension D and sets the state vector length to M = D + 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.EulerEquation"},{"category":"method","location":"PDEs/euler/#HyperCloudCore.is_admissible-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{EulerEquation{D, M, T, <:Conservative}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{M, T}}} where {D, M, T}","page":"Euler","text":"Admissible conservative Euler state (MOOD PAD): finite, positive density and pressure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloudCore.is_admissible"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#Kinetic-Relaxation","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"The kinetic relaxation approach replaces a nonlinear system of N_M conservation laws by N_K linear transport equations with a stiff source:\n\npartial_t f_k + sum_d=1^D lambda_kdpartial_x_d f_k = fracmathcalM_k(u) - f_kepsilon\nqquad u = textsum of the kinetic variables belonging to each macroscopic variable\n\nAs epsilon to 0 the macroscopic variables u satisfy the original conservation law. The transport part is a diagonal LinearAdvection system, so all spatial schemes work on it unchanged. The source is solved implicitly.","title":"Kinetic Relaxation"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#Setting-it-up","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"Use the kinetic pipeline with an IMEX time stepper and the Kinetic_ namespace:\n\nvels, idx = generate_relax_params(2.0, D, M)   # speeds ±2 along each axis\n\nparams[:sim_func_name]      = :run_kinetic_simulation\nparams[:Time_stepper]       = :ARS222\nparams[:Kinetic_velocities] = vels\nparams[:Kinetic_indices]    = idx\nparams[:Kinetic_epsilon]    = 1e-6\n\nThe kinetic speed must exceed the largest wave speed of the macroscopic system (subcharacteristic condition). For Burgers with u le 1, a speed of 2.0 is safe.","title":"Setting it up"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#Mapping-kinetic-to-macroscopic-variables-(Kin2Macro)","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"Kin2Macro is built from a list of edges. Kin2Macro([1, 3, 6]) assigns kinetic variables 1:2 to macroscopic variable 1 and 3:5 to variable 2.\n\nkm(f) sums each group and returns the macroscopic state.\nkm(k) returns the macroscopic index of kinetic variable k.\n\ngenerate_relax_params(v, D, M) produces 2D variables per macroscopic variable, with speeds pm v along each axis, and the matching edges.","title":"Mapping kinetic to macroscopic variables (Kin2Macro)"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#Equilibrium-and-source-term","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"For kinetic variable k belonging to macroscopic variable m the equilibrium is\n\nmathcalM_k(u) = c_mBig(u_m + sum_d=1^D F_d^m(u) s_kdBig)\nqquad\ns_kd = begincases gamma  lambda_kd  lambda_kd neq 0  0  textotherwiseendcases\n\nwhere c_m = 1  (textnumber of kinetic variables of  m), F_d^m is the physical flux, and gamma is interior_factor (default D). The constructor precomputes the s_kd and 1epsilon.\n\nFor the velocity sets from generate_relax_params with the default interior_factor, the equilibrium reproduces the macroscopic state and flux exactly:\n\nsum_k in m mathcalM_k(u) = u_m\nqquad\nsum_k in m lambda_kdmathcalM_k(u) = F_d^m(u)\n\nevaluate_source returns (mathcalM_k(u) - f_k)epsilon.","title":"Equilibrium and source term"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#Implicit-solve","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"In each IMEX stage with implicit coefficient a_ii, the stage value satisfies Y = Y^* + a_iiDelta t(mathcalM(u) - Y)epsilon. The relaxation does not change the macroscopic variables (sum_kin m(mathcalM_k - f_k) = 0), so u can be taken from Y^*, and implicit_solve is exact and explicit in form:\n\nY_k = fracY^*_k + fraca_iiDelta tepsilonmathcalM_k(u(Y^*))1 + fraca_iiDelta tepsilon \n\nThis holds for any epsilon  0, including the stiff limit.","title":"Implicit solve"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#Initialization","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"set_initial_conditions! has a kinetic method. It takes the macroscopic initial condition and sets every particle to the equilibrium mathcalM(u_0(x)), so you never write kinetic initial data by hand.","title":"Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#API","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#HyperCloud.Kin2Macro","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"Kin2Macro{NM, NK}\n\nA mapping structure bridging kinetic (NK) and macroscopic (NM) state components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.Kin2Macro"},{"category":"type","location":"SourceTerms/kinetic/#HyperCloud.RelaxationSourceTerm","page":"Kinetic Relaxation","text":"RelaxationSourceTerm{D, NM, NK, T, MEQ} <: AbstractImplicitSourceTerm\n\nModels the stiff local relaxation of a kinetic system toward the macroscopic Maxwellian equilibrium state.\n\nConstructors\n\nRelaxationSourceTerm(km, eps, coeffs, macro_eq, eq_kin, interior_factor): Precomputes the scaled inverse kinetic wave speeds utilizing the kinetic_wave_speed API, scaling them by the interior_factor (which defaults to the spatial dimension D). It stores the inverse of the relaxation parameter (eps) to optimize the runtime implicit solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.RelaxationSourceTerm"},{"category":"section","location":"#HyperCloud.jl","page":"Home","text":"HyperCloud.jl is a meshfree framework for hyperbolic conservation laws. It solves PDEs on particle clouds with moving-least-squares (MLS) reconstructions: first-order upwind, MUSCL up to fifth order, and WENO. Slope limiters and a posteriori MOOD limiting keep shocks stable. A kinetic relaxation pipeline with IMEX time stepping approximates nonlinear systems by linear transport plus a stiff source term.\n\nHyperCloud is part of the PDEStudio ecosystem:\n\nHyperCloudCore.jl provides the numerical engine (particle grids, MLS schemes, MOOD, time steppers, boundary conditions, GPU backends) and the config-driven builders that HyperCloud's runners call. HyperCloud re-exports it.\nPDEStudioCore.jl runs batches of simulations, caches results on disk under a hash of their parameters, and computes error statistics.\nPDEStudio.jl plots the results, either locally (GLMakie) or in the browser from a headless machine (WGLMakie + Bonito).","title":"HyperCloud.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-simulation","page":"Home","text":"A simulation is a function returning a SimulationConfig: shared parameters, a dictionary of numerical methods, and the methods to run.\n\nusing HyperCloud\n\nfunction my_advection()\n    shared = Dict{Symbol, Any}(\n        :sim_func_name => :run_direct_simulation,\n        :tmax => 1.0, :snapshots => 20,\n\n        :PDE_name => :linear, :PDE_velocities => ((1.0,),),   # u_t + u_x = 0\n\n        :Grid_domain => :rectangular, :Grid_mins => (0.0,), :Grid_maxs => (1.0,),\n        :Grid_Ns => (200,), :Grid_periodic => true,\n        :Grid_randomness_factor => (0.2,), :Grid_seed => 42, :Grid_mover => :none,\n\n        :Weight_name => :exponential, :Weight_alpha => 1.0, :Weight_range => 5.5,\n\n        :IC_name => :gauss, :IC_a => (1.0,), :IC_b => (0.5,), :IC_width => 0.1,\n\n        :Time_CFL => 0.2,\n    )\n\n    methods = Dict{Symbol, Dict{Symbol, Any}}(\n        :analytical_solution => Dict{Symbol, Any}(),\n        :RK2MUSCL2 => last(create_method(:RK2, :MUSCL, 2)),\n        :RK4MUSCL4 => last(create_method(:RK4, :MUSCL, 4)),\n    )\n\n    return SimulationConfig(shared, methods, [:analytical_solution, :RK2MUSCL2, :RK4MUSCL4];\n                            ref_func_name = :analytical_solution)\nend\n\n@sim my_advection     # run and cache\n@plot my_advection    # open the interactive viewer (needs PDEStudio and a Makie backend)\n\nEvery available key is listed in the Configuration Reference.","title":"A first simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Workflow","page":"Home","text":"Configure. Write a function returning a SimulationConfig, either in your script or as name.jl in one of the CONFIG_SEARCH_PATHS folders (see Execution Macros).\nRun. @sim name builds and runs every active method and caches the results.\nVisualize. @plot name loads the configuration into the PDEStudio GUI.\nArchive. @backup name copies the configuration file into a timestamped backups folder.","title":"Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-layout","page":"Home","text":"Folder / file Content\nPDEs/ Equation definitions (linear advection, Burgers, Euler) and their exact solutions.\nSourceTerms/ Source terms, currently the kinetic relaxation term.\nICs/ Initial conditions: Gaussian pulse, box, sine wave and three Riemann variants.\nBuilder/ The physics builders (build_equation, build_kinetic_system) and the exact references; the numerical builders come from HyperCloudCore (see Builder API).\ndevice.jl GPU placement and memory limit of the runners (storage_for, set_memory_limit!).\ninbuilt_methods.jl create_method, get_master_method_dict, generate_relax_params.\nSimulationFunctions/ The pipelines run_direct_simulation and run_kinetic_simulation.\ntime_integration.jl The time loop solve_equation and snapshot storage.\nBuilder/reference.jl analytical_solution and the analytic_closure interface.\nmacros.jl @sim, @plot, @backup.\nExamples/ Ready-to-run benchmarks: euler_1d_sod, burgers_kinetic, advection_2d.","title":"Package layout"},{"category":"section","location":"time_integration/#Time-Integration","page":"Time Integration","text":"solve_equation runs the time loop: it computes the step size, advances the particle grid with the chosen Runge–Kutta or IMEX stepper (HyperCloudCore's Time Stepper), applies particle adaptivity between steps if it is enabled, reports progress, and stores snapshots.","title":"Time Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"time_integration/#Time-step","page":"Time Integration","text":"Fixed: with is_cfl = false, dt is the physical time step.\nCFL: with is_cfl = true (:Time_CFL in a configuration), dt is a CFL number and the physical step is recomputed every iteration as dt * get_time_step(pg, eq, div_interp). This uses the current wave speeds of the equation and the MLS derivative stencils of the grid; see HyperCloudCore's CFL time step for the formula and measured limits.\n\nThe last step is shortened so the run ends exactly at tmax.","title":"Time step"},{"category":"section","location":"time_integration/#Snapshots","page":"Time Integration","text":"snapshots + 1 states are stored, at the target times range(0, tmax, length = snapshots + 1).\n\nSnapshot 1 is the initial state at t = 0.\nEach further snapshot is taken at the end of the first step that reaches its target time, so t[k] can lie slightly after the target.\nThe final state is always stored in the last slot.\n\nsaveData! copies positions and states into new arrays, so later steps do not modify stored snapshots. With remove_ghosts = true only non-boundary particles are kept. The runners pass :remove_ghosts from the configuration (default true); solve_equation itself defaults to false.","title":"Snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"time_integration/#Progress-output","page":"Time Integration","text":"show_progress = true: a progress bar.\nshow_progress = false: an @info line with the percentage and an ETA (HH:MM:SS) every progress_interval seconds, which is better suited for log files.\n\nThe runners take both settings from global switches: enable_progress_bar!(false) and set_progress_interval!(seconds).\n\nThe loop also logs the execution backend of the parallel loops (see set_backend!).","title":"Progress output"},{"category":"section","location":"time_integration/#Return-value","page":"Time Integration","text":"solve_equation returns (positions, states, times, k_step, elapsed_time): the stored positions, states and times per snapshot, the number of steps, and the wall-clock time of the loop in seconds.","title":"Return value"},{"category":"section","location":"time_integration/#API","page":"Time Integration","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"time_integration/#HyperCloud.solve_equation","page":"Time Integration","text":"solve_equation(timestepper, eq, pg, tmax, dt; kwargs...)\n\nThe primary simulation orchestrator governing the main time-stepping loop. \n\nArguments\n\ntimestepper::TimeStepper: The selected Runge-Kutta or IMEX time integrator.\neq::HyperbolicPDE: The physical equation system.\npg::ParticleGrid: The active mesh-free domain configuration.\ntmax::Real: The final simulation time.\ndt::Real: The baseline time step.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nis_cfl::Bool: If true, dt is treated as a CFL number, and the physical time step is dynamically computed at each iteration using the grid and interpolator properties.\nsnapshots::Integer: The number of discrete data dumps to record evenly across the simulation timeline.\nremove_ghosts::Bool: Strips boundary/ghost particles from the returned snapshot data if true.\nshow_progress::Bool: Toggles visual progress tracking.\nprogress_interval::Real: Sets the refresh rate (in seconds) for logging the simulation's progress and calculating the ETA.\nadaptivity: a ParticleAdaptivity applied between steps (particle insertion/merging on moving grids), or nothing.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple containing: (position_history, state_history, time_history, total_steps, elapsed_wall_time).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.solve_equation"},{"category":"function","location":"time_integration/#HyperCloud.saveData!","page":"Time Integration","text":"saveData!(xs_storage, us_storage, ts_storage, snap_idx, pg, current_t, remove_ghosts)\n\nExtracts and archives the simulation state at a specific snapshot index.\n\nDetails\n\nRecords the current time into ts_storage.\nIf remove_ghosts is true, it filters out boundary particles and saves only the active domain core. Otherwise, it copies the entire grid.\nAllocates new state and position vectors for the targeted snapshot and copies the corresponding views from the particle grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.saveData!"},{"category":"section","location":"macros/#Execution-Macros","page":"Execution Macros","text":"The macros are the main user interface. They resolve a configuration by name, run it, and handle plugin loading and world-age issues for you.","title":"Execution Macros"},{"category":"section","location":"macros/#Global-settings","page":"Execution Macros","text":"Variable Meaning\nCONFIG_SEARCH_PATHS Folders searched recursively for name.jl (default [\"configs\", \"experiments\"]).\nCONFIG_ROOT_OVERWRITE Project root for that search. Defaults to PDEStudioCore.get_save_path().\nBONITO_LISTEN_URL, BONITO_LISTEN_PORT, BONITO_PROXY_URL Server settings for browser plots with WGLMakie.\n\nAll of them are Refs, e.g. HyperCloud.CONFIG_SEARCH_PATHS[] = [\"my_cases\"].","title":"Global settings"},{"category":"section","location":"macros/#@sim","page":"Execution Macros","text":"@sim name\n@sim \"name\"\n@sim :name\n\nRuns the configuration returned by name():\n\nIf a function name exists in the calling module, it is used.\nOtherwise the macro searches CONFIG_SEARCH_PATHS for name.jl, includes it, and calls the function of the same name.\nThe call goes through Base.invokelatest, so functions defined by a freshly included file work in the same session.\n\nIf neither exists, @sim throws an ErrorException.\n\nnote: Failed runs do not abort a batch\nThe simulation runners catch exceptions, log them with @error, and return nothing. A batch therefore continues past a failing method; check the log for errors.","title":"@sim"},{"category":"section","location":"macros/#@plot","page":"Execution Macros","text":"@plot\n@plot name\n\nOpens the PDEStudio viewer. With name, the configuration is resolved like in @sim, passed to the viewer with set_sim_config!, and computed (or loaded from cache).\n\nThe backend is chosen from what is loaded in the calling module:\n\nGLMakie: a local window.\nWGLMakie: plots are served by a Bonito server (configured once per session with the BONITO_* settings). If Bonito itself is not loaded, a warning is printed.\nNeither: a warning is printed and the figure is returned without being displayed.","title":"@plot"},{"category":"section","location":"macros/#@backup","page":"Execution Macros","text":"@backup name\n\nCopies the file that defines name into a backups folder next to it, with a timestamp in the file name (name_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.jl). Use it to archive the exact configuration behind a result.","title":"@backup"},{"category":"section","location":"macros/#API","page":"Execution Macros","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"macro","location":"macros/#HyperCloud.@sim","page":"Execution Macros","text":"@sim name\n\nEvaluates the configuration and runs the simulation. Checks the local module scope first;  if not found, dynamically loads the configuration from the user's configured search paths. Accepts strings (\"name\"), bare words (name), or symbols (:name).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.@sim"},{"category":"macro","location":"macros/#HyperCloud.@plot","page":"Execution Macros","text":"@plot()\n\nLaunches the PDEStudio plotter utilizing the active module's backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n@plot name\n\nLaunches the PDEStudio plotter, loads the configuration from the function or plugin name, and forces a plot update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.@plot"},{"category":"macro","location":"macros/#HyperCloud.@backup","page":"Execution Macros","text":"@backup name\n\nManually triggers a backup of the source file where the configuration function or script name is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HyperCloud.@backup"}]
}
